[inchang-ing 2026-10-29] I can reproduce this on master (`2.5.0+dev` @ `f501cd0`):

```python
def f(x: int | None) -> bool:
    return x >= 0
```

```
main.py:2: error: Unsupported operand types for <= ("int" and "None")  [operator]
main.py:2: note: Left operand is of type "int | None"
```

Root cause: the message is rendered in `mypy/messages.py:646` (`incompatible_argument`, the `elif name.startswith('"__')` branch) purely from the name of the method that failed:

```python
for method, op in op_methods_to_symbols.items():
    for variant in method, "__r" + method[2:]:
        if name.startswith(f'"{variant}" of'):
```

